    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Medical Physiology MCQ Examination Bank</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Comprehensive Assessment Coverings for All Detailed Respiratory Physiology Modules</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Page 1: Basal Metabolic Rates, External Respiration & Functional Airway Anatomy</div>
        
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">1. What is the average normal oxygen consumption (O₂ consumption) of a resting adult body per minute?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 150 ml/min</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> 200 ml/min</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> 250 ml/min</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> 300 ml/min</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">2. How much carbon dioxide (CO₂ production) is normally produced by human body tissues during rest?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> 200 ml/min</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> 250 ml/min</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 150 ml/min</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> 100 ml/min</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">3. Which of the following processes is NOT categorized as a phase of External Respiration?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Pulmonary ventilation</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Pulmonary gas exchange</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Transport of gases by blood</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Intracellular O₂ utilization by mitochondria</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">4. Structurally and functionally, the dynamic respiratory system is composed of:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Air passages, alveoli, lungs, chest wall, and respiratory muscles</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Conducting zones and alveolar sacs exclusively</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> The pulmonary capillaries and cardiac output structures</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Diaphragm muscle and higher cerebral cortices only</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">5. Which nervous structures contain the primary centers directly responsible for rhythmic respiratory control?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cerebellum</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Brain stem (Medulla and Pons)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Sympathetic collateral ganglia</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Lumbar spinal cord segments</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">6. Which structural section belongs entirely to the Upper Airway classification?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Trachea</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Larynx</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Pharynx (Nasal, Oral, Laryngeal parts)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Terminal bronchioles</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">7. The Lower Airway includes all of the following physiological components EXCEPT:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Nasal Cavity</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Larynx</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Trachea</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Bronchi and Bronchioles</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">8. What are the three defined physiological divisions of the pharynx involved in upper air conduction?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Anterior, middle, posterior pharynx</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Nasal, oral, and laryngeal pharynx</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Esophageal, tracheal, and tympanic pharynx</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Vestibular, glottic, and subglottic pharynx</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">9. The nasal cavity is functionally linked to which vital protective airway reflex?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cough reflex</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Hering-Breuer reflex</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Sneezing reflex</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Swallowing reflex</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">10. Irritation within the larynx and trachea triggers which localized protective reflex mechanism?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Cough reflex</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Sneezing reflex</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Gag reflex</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Apneustic gasp reflex</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">Page 1 Answer Key 📍 1: C | 2: A | 3: D | 4: A | 5: B | 6: C | 7: A | 8: B | 9: C | 10: A</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <span>Faculty of Human Medicine - Suez University</span>
                <span>Course Instructor: Dr. Ghada Salah El-Dekroury</span>
            </div>
