    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-title">Page 2: Bronchial Tree, Non-Respiratory Functions & Function Tests Assessments</div>
        
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">11. What is the average normal length of the adult human trachea?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 5 to 8 cm</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> 10 to 12 cm</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 15 to 18 cm</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> 20 to 25 cm</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">12. Functionally, the bronchial tree branches out into which three specific metabolic zones?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Conducting, Transitional, and Respiratory zones</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Upper, Middle, and Lower zones</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Filtration, Humidification, and Expulsion zones</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Secretory, Elastic, and Rigid zones</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">13. The pleural cavity is a fluid-filled potential space. What is its standard fluid thickness?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 0.5 to 1 cm</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> 1.5 to 2 cm</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 3 to 4 mm</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> A thin layer of a few micrometers (liquid film)</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">14. Non-respiratory functions of the lungs include which specific groups of tasks?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Gastric acid buffering and structural calcium storage</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Protective, Metabolic, and Endocrine functions</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Hepatic enzyme secretion and voluntary locomotor patterns</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Lymphatic filtration of systemic chyme exclusively</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">15. The respiratory protective mechanism fundamentally splits into:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Role of air passages & Role of alveoli</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Role of tonsils & Role of white blood cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Role of standard saliva & Role of alveolar surfactant</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Role of costal cartilage & Role of mucus glands</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">16. Which medical conditions are listed under pathophysiological alterations of Bronchial Tone?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Pulmonary Fibrosis and Pneumonia</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Asthma and Emphysema</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Tuberculosis and Atelectasis</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Pulmonary Embolism and ARDS</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">17. Which parameter is NOT included in the listed "Assessment of Pulmonary Functions" guidelines?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Lung Compliance and Pulmonary ventilation</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Direct partial pressure measurement of blood Nitrogen content</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Dead space evaluation and non-functional alveoli</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Respiratory reserve index and Timed Vital Capacity</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">18. Which specific lung volume holds significant medicolegal importance (e.g., proving live birth)?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Tidal volume (TV)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV)</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Minimal air (part of Residual Volume)</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">19. Which of the following factors was NOT listed as affecting Vital Capacity and Timed Vital Capacity?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Posture, movement of the diaphragm, and pulmonary blood volume</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Strength of respiratory muscles and lung compliance/elasticity</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Baseline systemic hemoglobin concentrations and heart rate</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Airway resistance and chest wall expandability</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">20. What is the precise definition of Respiratory Dead Space?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> The volume of inspired air that does not take part in gas exchange</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> The amount of air remaining in lungs after maximal forced expiration</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Non-functional spaces resulting from total collapse of the lungs</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> The volume of air exhaled within the first second of expiration</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">Page 2 Answer Key 📍 11: B | 12: A | 13: D | 14: B | 15: A | 16: B | 17: B | 18: D | 19: C | 20: A</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <span>Faculty of Human Medicine - Suez University</span>
                <span>Course Instructor: Dr. Ghada Salah El-Dekroury</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-title">Page 3: Anatomical vs Physiological Dead Space & Alveolar Ventilation Dynamics</div>
        
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">21. Dead space ventilation is termed "wasted ventilation" and is primarily categorized into:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Inspiratory dead space and Expiratory dead space</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Anatomical dead space and Physiological dead space</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Nasal dead space and Tracheobronchial dead space</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Pathological dead space and Therapeutic dead space</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">22. What constitutes the Anatomical Dead Space?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> The volume of non-perfused collapsed alveolar sacs</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> The volume of conducting air passages (nose to terminal bronchioles) where no gas exchange occurs</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> The total air trapped inside the visceral pleural layers</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Total volume of air inspired minus the residual volume value</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">23. What is the normal physiological value of Anatomical Dead Space in a healthy adult?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 50 ml</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> 100 ml</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> 150 ml</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> 500 ml</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">24. Around what percentage of a standard resting Tidal Volume (500 ml) does the Anatomical Dead Space represent?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 10%</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> 30%</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 50%</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> 70%</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">25. How is the total Physiological Dead Space calculated?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Anatomical Dead Space + Alveolar Dead Space</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Tidal Volume - Anatomical Dead Space</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Vital Capacity divided by Respiratory Rate</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Expiratory Reserve Volume + Minimal Air volume</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">26. What causes the development of Alveolar Dead Space?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Extreme bronchoconstriction during severe acute asthma</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Alveoli that are ventilated but have inadequate or completely absent blood perfusion</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Total absence of regional ventilation with completely normal blood flow</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Excessive structural thickening of the respiratory membrane layers</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">27. In which clinical conditions does Physiological Dead Space increase significantly compared to Anatomical Dead Space?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Iron deficiency anemia and systemic cyanide toxicosis</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Pulmonary embolism, decreased cardiac output, Emphysema, and ARDS</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Raynaud's phenomenon and localized frostbite exposures</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Mild allergic rhinitis and subacute tracheitis</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">28. Which of the following mathematical equations correctly determines the Alveolar Ventilation Rate per minute?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Alveolar Ventilation = Tidal Volume &times; Respiratory Rate</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Alveolar Ventilation = Dead Space Volume &times; Respiratory Rate</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Alveolar Ventilation = (Tidal Volume + Dead Space Volume) &times; Respiratory Rate</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Alveolar Ventilation = (Tidal Volume - Dead Space Volume) &times; Respiratory Rate</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">29. What is pulmonary surfactant and which specific cellular components synthesize it?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> A complex mix of lipids/proteins secreted by Type II Alveolar epithelial cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> A specialized digestive fluid released by active alveolar macrophages</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> A structural plasma carrier synthesized by Type I Alveolar cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> An endocrine hormone manufactured by local neuroendocrine airway tissues</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">30. What is the fundamental, primary biophysical function of pulmonary surfactant within the alveoli?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Increasing surface tension forces to aid passive lung recoil pathways</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Decreasing surface tension at the air-fluid interface to prevent alveolar collapse during expiration</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Mechanically filtering out macromolecular dust particles and pollens</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Directly converting carbonic acid into gaseous carbon dioxide molecules</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">Page 3 Answer Key 📍 21: B | 22: B | 23: C | 24: B | 25: A | 26: B | 27: B | 28: D | 29: A | 30: B</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <span>Faculty of Human Medicine - Suez University</span>
                <span>Course Instructor: Dr. Ghada Salah El-Dekroury</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-title">Page 4: Surfactant Biochemistry, Clinical Syndromes & Lung Compliance Variations</div>
        
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">31. Functions of the pulmonary surfactant include all of the following physiological actions EXCEPT:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Stabilizes the size of alveoli and prevents them from collapsing</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Lowers the work of breathing by increasing overall lung compliance</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Keeps alveoli dry and protects against the development of pulmonary edema</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Increases systemic arterial vascular resistance to speed up flow rates</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">32. Which specific phospholipid represents the major active component of surfactant responsible for lowering surface tension?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Sphingomyelin molecules</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Phosphatidylserine complexes</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Cephalin phospholipid fractions</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">33. At around which week of gestational age (pregnancy) does surfactant synthesis normally begin in the fetal lung?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Week 12</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Week 20</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Week 28</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Week 36</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">34. Premature infants born before sufficient surfactant production suffer from which disease, and what is the standard therapy?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome (NRDS); treated with exogenous surfactant instillation</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Idiopathic pulmonary sclerosis; managed with high-dose broad antibiotics</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Congenital pulmonary emphysema; managed with forced expiratory breathing maneuvers</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Stagnant tissue hypoxia; managed via active aspiration of intrapleural secretions</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">35. Which clinical conditions can severely damage or inhibit surfactant production in adult populations?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Severe lung trauma, localized severe infection, or Adult Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Chronic microcytic anemia due to iron deficiency syndromes</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Localized nitrogen gas toxicosis following scuba ascents</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> High-intensity aerobic workouts at moderate mountain heights</div>
            </div>
