                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It is perfectly S-shaped and shifts rightward during respiration</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">72. Which physiological variables are plotted on the X-axis and Y-axis of the CO₂ dissociation curve?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> X-axis: pH; Y-axis: Total O₂ content volume</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> X-axis: PCO₂ (mmHg); Y-axis: Total CO₂ content of the blood (ml/100ml)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> X-axis: PO₂ pressure; Y-axis: SpO₂ percentage lines</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> X-axis: Respiratory Rate; Y-axis: Alveolar Ventilation volume</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">73. What are the three primary forms of CO₂ transport in blood and their approximate percentages?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Dissolved (80%), bound to hemoglobin (15%), as bicarbonate (5%)</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> As Bicarbonate (75-80%), Carbaminohemoglobin (10-20%), and Dissolved gas (5-10%)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Free gas state (50%), carbonic acid form (40%), iron linked (10%)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Sodium bicarbonate form (99%) and dissolved inside leucocytes (1%)</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">74. What is the Haldane Effect?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Deoxygenated blood has an increased capacity to carry CO₂; oxygenation of blood in the lungs promotes CO₂ unloading</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Temperature modifications driving carbon monoxide dissociation kinetics</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Elevated oxygen binding capabilities following drops in tissue proton numbers</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Sudden alveolar membrane failure secondary to pulmonary thrombosis</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">75. How many distinct clinical types of Hypoxia are identified within the course syllabus?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Two forms</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Three forms</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Four forms (4 Types)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Five forms</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">76. What are the correct medical names of the four specific types of tissue Hypoxia?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Acute, Chronic, Congenital, and Acquired Hypoxias</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Hypoxemic type, Anemic type, Stagnant type, Histotoxic type</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Respiratory, Cardiac, Vascular, and Cellular Hypoxias</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Central, Peripheral, Localized, and Generalized Hypoxias</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">77. What is the definition of Hypoxemic Hypoxia (Hypoxemia)?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Total deficit in functional hemoglobin counts despite normal gas pressures</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Reduced arterial PO₂, resulting in inadequate oxygenation of the hemoglobin</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Extreme slowing or pooling inside peripheral systemic capillary beds</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Cell enzyme poisonings preventing utilization of perfectly normal oxygen supplies</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">78. What are the two main mechanisms causing Hypoxemic Hypoxia?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Advanced systemic anemia or severe acute hemorrhage events</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Cyanide toxicosis or direct gaseous carbon monoxide poisoning profiles</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> (1) Decreased PO₂ in inspired air (high altitudes/aviators) or (2) Cardiopulmonary failure (lung disease/shunts)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Localized tourniquet inflation or vascular spasms in peripheral limbs</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">79. What defines Anemic Hypoxia, and what are its major medical etiologies?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Reduced blood flow rates owing to sudden acute congestive heart failure</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Decreased oxygen-carrying capacity due to low functional Hb; caused by anemia, hemorrhage, CO poisoning, or methemoglobinemia</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Drop in environmental barometric pressure secondary to high mountain climbing</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Total failure of alveolar structures to synthesize functional surfactant batches</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">80. What is the definition of Stagnant (Hypokinetic) Hypoxia?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Severe inactivation of oxidative enzyme complexes within mitochondria</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Fall in arterial oxygen pressure lines caused by renal-respiratory errors</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Arterial PO₂ and total oxygen are normal, but blood flow to tissues is slowed or inadequate</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Absolute block in gas diffusions across the alveolar capillary barriers</div>
            </div>
        </div>

