    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-title">Page 9: Stagnant Subtypes, Cyanosis Thresholds, Clinical Classes & Tetralogy of Fallot</div>
        
        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">81. Stagnant hypoxia can be General or Local. What are the specific causes of General Stagnant Hypoxia?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Heart diseases weakening circulation, poor venous return, or shock states</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Raynaud's syndrome, Buerger's disease, and local tourniquets exclusively</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Severe acute blood loss hemorrhage and pernicious macrocytic anemia</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> High-altitude flights inside unpressurized aircraft cabins lacking O₂ gear</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">82. Which of the following is a recognized cause of Local Stagnant Hypoxia?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Congestive heart failure and general cardiovascular shock</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Raynaud's disease, Buerger's disease, tourniquets, ergot poisoning, and exposure to cold</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Cyanide toxicity blocking cellular utilization pathways completely</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Inherited sickle cell anemia crises displaying universal pain signs</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">83. What is Histotoxic Hypoxia and what classic biochemical example is provided?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Inability of blood to carry oxygen due to severe structural iron lack</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Venous pooling within distal extremities brought on by freezing exposures</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Tissues cannot utilize oxygen despite normal delivery and pressure; caused by Cyanide poisoning</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Structural damage to Type I alveolar cells following severe chest compression</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">84. What is the absolute physiological threshold for Cyanosis to become clinically visible?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Accumulation of 2 gm of reduced hemoglobin per 100 ml of capillary blood</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Presence of 5 gm of reduced hemoglobin per 100 ml of capillary blood</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Drop in arterial saturation percentage (SpO₂) under a fixed 80% mark</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Presence of more than 10 gm of carbaminohemoglobin compounds in veins</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">85. Clinical presentation of Cyanosis is categorized into which two major classifications?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Acute and Chronic Cyanosis</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Central (generalized) and Peripheral (localized) Cyanosis</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Pulmonary-driven and Cardiac-driven Cyanosis only</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Congenital neonatal and Acquired histotoxic Cyanosis</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">86. Central cyanosis occurs in respiratory ventilation errors and cardiac anomalies like septal defects (ASD/VSD) or mixed disorders such as:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Raynaud's disease</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Tetralogy of Fallot</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Buerger's thromboangiitis obliterans</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Isolated infant respiratory distress syndrome variation</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">87. What are the four specific anatomical cardiac anomalies that define Tetralogy of Fallot?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD), Pulmonary Stenosis, Overriding Aorta, and Right Ventricular Hypertrophy</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Atrial Septal Defect, Mitral Stenosis, Aortic Coarctation, and Left Ventricular Hypertrophy</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Patent Ductus Arteriosus, Tricuspid Atresia, Overriding Pulmonary Trunk, and Cardiomyopathy</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> VSD, Aortic Insufficiency, Left Ventricular Dilation, and Bacterial Endocarditis</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">88. Which clinical entity represents a classic example of Peripheral (localized) Cyanosis?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Congenital Tetralogy of Fallot</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Large isolated Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD)</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Raynaud's disease (triggered by cold exposure or emotion)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Extreme respiratory failure during a status asthmaticus attack</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">89. What are the three broad physiological causes of Cyanosis listed in the text's summaries?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Hypoxic hypoxia, Stagnant hypoxia, and Asphyxia</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Anemic hypoxia, Histotoxic hypoxia, and systemic shock</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Cyanide poisoning, Carbon monoxide poisoning, and Uremia</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Pulmonary Fibrosis, Asthma, and Emphysema</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">90. What are the four distinct physical factors that affect the clinical appearance of Cyanosis?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Ambient room temperature, peripheral pulse rate, systemic cardiac output, and body weight</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Capillary factor, Skin thickness, Skin pigmentation, and Blood composition</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Patient height, gender markers, genetic genotypes, and baseline core metabolic rates</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Total surfactant volume, static lung compliance, and intrapleural pressure values</div>
            </div>
        </div>

